<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">&nbsp;</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt"><FONT STYLE="font-size: 10pt">[2:13]</FONT> What we&rsquo;re
seeing now, it&rsquo;s very different. We&rsquo;re seeing network hash rate, we hit close to 1.3 zettahashes late last year, and it&rsquo;s been gradually,
still gradually, coming down. This has been the longest period that we&rsquo;ve seen from an all-time high until recovery.</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">&nbsp;</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt"><FONT STYLE="font-size: 10pt">[2:36]</FONT> Back to Bitcoin
mining. We hear this a lot. I think there are 2 big accusations that we hear about Bitcoin mining. One, that it&rsquo;s bad business. And two,
that it&rsquo;s wasted energy.</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">&nbsp;</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt"><FONT STYLE="font-size: 10pt">[2:50]</FONT> Let me start
with the first one, that Bitcoin mining is a bad business. And commodity businesses, they don&rsquo;t come in good and bad, right? They come
in where you are in the cost curve. If you&rsquo;re extracting a commodity at very high cost, yes, it is gonna be a bad business for you. But
if you&rsquo;re at the left side of the cost curve, that could be a very profitable business and potentially extremely healthy.</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">&nbsp;</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt"><FONT STYLE="font-size: 10pt">[3:18]</FONT> Bitcoin is
a very unique commodity when you think about commodities, right? First of all, just like oil, it is fungible, but I think it&rsquo;s the purest
form of commodity that we&rsquo;ve ever seen. Satoshi embedded in code the difficulty adjustment, which basically means that no matter how much
more hash rate you have out there, on average, a block is still going to be mined every 10 minutes. That&rsquo;s very different, because when
you compare, for example, with oil or gold, typically higher prices lead to more development, to more mining in different places, which
eventually leads to more supply hitting the market and prices eventually coming down. In Bitcoin mining, it doesn&rsquo;t matter. We can turn
off our hashing machines that we currently have, the market wouldn&rsquo;t feel it. In 2 weeks, we&rsquo;re gonna have a new difficulty adjustment.</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt; text-align: justify">&nbsp;</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt; text-align: justify"><FONT STYLE="font-size: 10pt">[4:11]</FONT>
And I think when we think about how many, how, you know, why many people failed in mining in the past, it&rsquo;s much more about their capital
structure and their cost structure than it is about hash price, about Bitcoin price, and about where we are in the network.</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">&nbsp;</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt"><FONT STYLE="font-size: 10pt">[4:29]</FONT> Just look at
an example. If you pick two miners, and if you look at their different unit economics, imagine they just bought a fleet of ASICs. One
has very low cost of energy, very efficient machines. The other one has higher cost of energy and less efficient machines. What it means
in terms of, that&rsquo;s the difference between having very high margins and having to shut down your production.</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">&nbsp;</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt"><FONT STYLE="font-size: 10pt">[4:56]</FONT> And the market,
of course, improved from where it was, but hash price is still, compared to historical levels, relatively low.</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">&nbsp;</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt"><FONT STYLE="font-size: 10pt">[5:07]</FONT> Big question
we get all the time, and by the way, on Twenty One Capital, the first thing we think about as we&rsquo;re thinking about opportunities is, how
does that opportunity perform compared to Bitcoin? Bitcoin is our standard. Bitcoin is our benchmark, and if you can&rsquo;t beat Bitcoin, investors
should just be buying an ETF at the end of the day, because that&rsquo;s less risky than holding an operating business. And in mining, we get
that question all the time about, if I have a dollar, should I be looking to invest in mining, or should I be buying Bitcoin?</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">&nbsp;</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt"><FONT STYLE="font-size: 10pt">[5:39]</FONT> And the answer
is, if you only have $1, buy Bitcoin first. I think that&rsquo;s the best way to express your view. But in a diversification, if you&rsquo;re looking
at investing in larger scale, mining can make a ton of sense.</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">&nbsp;</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt"><FONT STYLE="font-size: 10pt">[5:56]</FONT> Fundamentally,
when you think about Bitcoin mining right at the end of the day, and I&rsquo;m gonna simplify here a ton, Bitcoin mining is gonna outperform
Bitcoin in a scenario where Bitcoin price goes up faster than network hash rate. So if Bitcoin price is going up by 50, 100 percent a
year, and network hash rate is flat, you have a much higher chance of outperforming.</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">&nbsp;</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt"><FONT STYLE="font-size: 10pt">[6:27]</FONT> I think for
a company, as I was saying, the right decision was never about buying Bitcoin or mining. The better risk adjusted allocation is to have
a little bit of both.</P>

<P STYLE="font: 10pt Times New Roman, Times, Serif; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">&nbsp;</P>


